WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.389 --> 00:00:03.027
- He's an actor, screen
writer, and director

00:00:03.027 --> 00:00:06.008
best known for creating
the Rocky and Rambo series.

00:00:06.008 --> 00:00:08.341
He was evicted from his
apartment and was homeless

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:11.213
for three weeks before getting
into the film industry.

00:00:11.213 --> 00:00:13.558
He became the third man
in history to receive

00:00:13.558 --> 00:00:16.263
nominations for Best Original Screenplay

00:00:16.263 --> 00:00:18.588
and Best Actor for the same film.

00:00:18.588 --> 00:00:21.204
He's Sylvester Stallone,
and here's his top 10

00:00:21.204 --> 00:00:22.704
rules for success.

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:29.335
- [Interviewer] What in this
whole world turns you on?

00:00:29.335 --> 00:00:33.836
- Challenge, I really,
I just love, I love it.

00:00:33.836 --> 00:00:38.003
I love being told no, it
just means yes, you know.

00:00:39.454 --> 00:00:40.522
- [Interviewer] To you.

00:00:40.522 --> 00:00:41.475
- Yeah.

00:00:41.475 --> 00:00:43.119
- What turns you off?

00:00:43.119 --> 00:00:45.750
- Lack of enthusiasm,
that really turns me off.

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:47.090
The point is I'm pursing
something and nobody

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:49.726
looks too happy about it.

00:00:49.726 --> 00:00:51.317
- But we're just looking
out for your interests.

00:00:51.317 --> 00:00:52.836
- I appreciate that, but
maybe you're looking out

00:00:52.836 --> 00:00:55.143
for your interests just a little bit more.

00:00:55.143 --> 00:00:57.275
I mean you shouldn't be asking
people to come down here

00:00:57.275 --> 00:00:59.298
and pay the freight on
something they paid,

00:00:59.298 --> 00:01:00.625
and still aren't good enough.

00:01:00.625 --> 00:01:01.837
I mean you think that's right?

00:01:01.837 --> 00:01:03.034
I mean maybe you're doing your job,

00:01:03.034 --> 00:01:05.537
but why you got to stop
me from doing mine?

00:01:05.537 --> 00:01:07.344
Cause if you're willing to
go through all the battling

00:01:07.344 --> 00:01:09.350
you got to go through to get
to where you want to get,

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:11.237
who's got the right to stop you?

00:01:11.237 --> 00:01:12.813
I mean maybe some of
you guys got something

00:01:12.813 --> 00:01:14.943
you never finished, something
you really want to do,

00:01:14.943 --> 00:01:16.901
something you never said
to somebody, something!

00:01:16.901 --> 00:01:18.836
And you're told no, even
after you paid your dues.

00:01:18.836 --> 00:01:20.721
Who's got the right to tell you that?

00:01:20.721 --> 00:01:21.803
Who, nobody.

00:01:21.803 --> 00:01:23.035
It's your right to listen to your gut,

00:01:23.035 --> 00:01:25.233
it ain't nobody's right
to say no after you earned

00:01:25.233 --> 00:01:26.401
the right to be where you want to be

00:01:26.401 --> 00:01:28.651
and do what you want to do.

00:01:32.194 --> 00:01:33.628
You know the older I get, the more things

00:01:33.628 --> 00:01:36.381
I got to leave behind, that's life.

00:01:36.381 --> 00:01:38.812
The only thing I'm asking you
guys to leave on the table

00:01:38.812 --> 00:01:40.660
is what's right.

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:42.551
We were running behind schedule,

00:01:42.551 --> 00:01:45.634
and I had to shoot a montage, oh God.

00:01:47.646 --> 00:01:51.224
This montage would have
taken like five days.

00:01:51.224 --> 00:01:54.362
So what I did is I came
in there, brought everyone

00:01:54.362 --> 00:01:58.587
into one big room, all the
dancers, and had three cameras.

00:01:58.587 --> 00:02:01.743
I took one camera and just
stayed with John Travolta,

00:02:01.743 --> 00:02:02.991
and I worked with him.

00:02:02.991 --> 00:02:05.362
And the rest I said to
everyone just dance,

00:02:05.362 --> 00:02:07.632
and do whatever you want, cart wheels.

00:02:07.632 --> 00:02:09.660
I said catch it all, whatever.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:13.726
And then I sent a camera down
when they're making the stage

00:02:13.726 --> 00:02:17.363
and just regular light, and
just shoot what you can.

00:02:17.363 --> 00:02:21.320
Just no sound or nothing,
and we put it all together,

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:23.423
and saved four days of filming.

00:02:23.423 --> 00:02:27.688
Which none of that, absolutely
zero, was choreographed.

00:02:27.688 --> 00:02:28.845
Isn't it funny?

00:02:28.845 --> 00:02:30.990
I said okay John just do this, cut.

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:32.108
Do this, cut.

00:02:32.108 --> 00:02:33.596
(laughter)

00:02:33.596 --> 00:02:35.037
I swear to God.

00:02:35.037 --> 00:02:37.057
And then the other dancers
were just over in the corner,

00:02:37.057 --> 00:02:38.884
one was just scratching,
doing a somersault,

00:02:38.884 --> 00:02:41.166
stretching, and you put it all together.

00:02:41.166 --> 00:02:42.548
- It's the magic of a myth.

00:02:42.548 --> 00:02:46.048
- Yeah, so there are no rules, it's crazy.

00:02:46.931 --> 00:02:50.477
I first met Bob Chartoff
and Irwin Winkler,

00:02:50.477 --> 00:02:54.144
and I believe I was
there on a casting call.

00:02:55.146 --> 00:02:57.096
So we're talking a little
bit, and I said I really

00:02:57.096 --> 00:02:59.768
wasn't right for the acting part.

00:02:59.768 --> 00:03:03.675
And on the way out, I said
I don't know if it matters,

00:03:03.675 --> 00:03:04.950
but I do a little bit of writing.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:05.783
And he goes, really?

00:03:05.783 --> 00:03:07.729
And I says yeah, I'm writing this story,

00:03:07.729 --> 00:03:10.908
I have this thing about
wrestlers and I might

00:03:10.908 --> 00:03:13.077
do something about boxing.

00:03:13.077 --> 00:03:14.630
He goes, well bring it around.

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:17.981
And I thought if I hadn't
stopped on the way out,

00:03:17.981 --> 00:03:21.012
you know that's why I tell
all actors or writers,

00:03:21.012 --> 00:03:22.864
don't give up, keep talking,
eventually you might

00:03:22.864 --> 00:03:27.393
hit a nerve somewhere
and they go come on back.

00:03:27.393 --> 00:03:31.092
And if they didn't say come
on back or bring it later

00:03:31.092 --> 00:03:32.740
and let's see what you developed,

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:34.207
I wouldn't be sitting here.

00:03:34.207 --> 00:03:38.543
What I'll try to do is
I write long handed,

00:03:38.543 --> 00:03:42.741
so on legal pad I'll go,
goes here, sees his mother,

00:03:42.741 --> 00:03:47.422
goes to the store, so
I'll have like ten things,

00:03:47.422 --> 00:03:49.901
of beats where he's going.

00:03:49.901 --> 00:03:52.483
Gets on a horse, goes
swimming, boom, boom, boom.

00:03:52.483 --> 00:03:56.765
And I do that, and I know in
my heart, 80 percent of it

00:03:56.765 --> 00:04:01.191
will be no good, but you're
getting through the screenplay.

00:04:01.191 --> 00:04:03.558
You're getting it done because the rewrite

00:04:03.558 --> 00:04:06.732
is always more fun and
much more enjoyable.

00:04:06.732 --> 00:04:09.341
I don't believe people
should look for perfection,

00:04:09.341 --> 00:04:13.174
or even 50 percent perfection
in the first draft.

00:04:13.174 --> 00:04:16.347
It's always going to be
kind of this like a child

00:04:16.347 --> 00:04:18.925
scribbling, but you did it.

00:04:18.925 --> 00:04:21.288
And the main thing for
me is to once you have

00:04:21.288 --> 00:04:23.958
that accomplished, and you
can do it in two weeks,

00:04:23.958 --> 00:04:27.364
three weeks, again, maybe
10 percent will be good,

00:04:27.364 --> 00:04:30.839
but you now have this
sense of accomplishment.

00:04:30.839 --> 00:04:33.457
You did it, and the
rewriting process starts,

00:04:33.457 --> 00:04:36.004
and that's when the fun begins.

00:04:36.004 --> 00:04:38.523
Originally when I brought
the script to them,

00:04:38.523 --> 00:04:40.639
they were fairly enthusiastic about it.

00:04:40.639 --> 00:04:42.726
The one thing they were
not enthusiastic about

00:04:42.726 --> 00:04:45.146
was me playing the part,
and I really can't blame

00:04:45.146 --> 00:04:47.073
them at the time.

00:04:47.073 --> 00:04:51.160
Ryan O'Neal was a
candidate, Burt Reynolds,

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:53.960
Robert Redford, Jimmy
Kahn, and they all were

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:55.934
at the top of their game.

00:04:55.934 --> 00:04:57.996
And so I could see it, but
there was something inside

00:04:57.996 --> 00:05:00.643
of me that you know this
opportunity is never

00:05:00.643 --> 00:05:02.183
going to come around.

00:05:02.183 --> 00:05:04.015
And I really wasn't used to money,

00:05:04.015 --> 00:05:06.413
and I had no idea what I would be missing.

00:05:06.413 --> 00:05:09.541
But the temptation
started to come forward.

00:05:09.541 --> 00:05:12.541
First it was 25 grand then $100,000,

00:05:14.678 --> 00:05:16.216
and I go I've never heard of 100,000.

00:05:16.216 --> 00:05:18.540
Because I had like &amp;160 in the bank,

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:20.619
and like I said I had to sell my dog,

00:05:20.619 --> 00:05:23.234
and things were not
looking very, very good.

00:05:23.234 --> 00:05:25.442
My $40 car I had just blown up,

00:05:25.442 --> 00:05:27.517
so I was taking a bus to work.

00:05:27.517 --> 00:05:30.107
And still it didn't matter,
I wanted to stick with it.

00:05:30.107 --> 00:05:32.685
Then it went up to 150,000, 175,000,

00:05:32.685 --> 00:05:34.578
then it went up to 250,000.

00:05:34.578 --> 00:05:37.240
Now my head was starting to spin.

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:41.157
And it went up to 330,000,
and probably I heard

00:05:42.567 --> 00:05:45.283
it went to 360,000, and I thought

00:05:45.283 --> 00:05:48.966
alright you know you've
really managed poverty

00:05:48.966 --> 00:05:50.907
very well, you've got
this down to a science,

00:05:50.907 --> 00:05:52.932
you really don't need much to live on.

00:05:52.932 --> 00:05:55.090
I had like sort of figured it out.

00:05:55.090 --> 00:05:58.923
So I was not in any way
used to the good life.

00:06:02.833 --> 00:06:07.050
So you know what, I know
in the back of my mind

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:12.018
if I sell this script and
it does very, very well,

00:06:12.018 --> 00:06:14.537
I'm going to jump off a
building if I'm not in it.

00:06:14.537 --> 00:06:15.846
There's no doubt about
it, I'm going to leap

00:06:15.846 --> 00:06:19.699
in front of a train, I'm
going to be very, very upset.

00:06:19.699 --> 00:06:22.557
So this is one of those things
where you just roll the dice,

00:06:22.557 --> 00:06:25.433
and you fly by the adverbial
seat of your pants,

00:06:25.433 --> 00:06:30.385
and say I got to try it, just
do it, I may be totally wrong,

00:06:30.385 --> 00:06:32.484
and I'm going to be taking a
lot of people down with me,

00:06:32.484 --> 00:06:34.371
but I just believe in it.

00:06:34.371 --> 00:06:38.351
Writing I almost oppose it like exercise.

00:06:38.351 --> 00:06:41.201
I get up maybe at 4:30 in the morning,

00:06:41.201 --> 00:06:42.550
it takes an hour to get going,

00:06:42.550 --> 00:06:46.717
and then I'll start to write
about maybe three hours.

00:06:48.467 --> 00:06:50.187
And then it becomes repetitive.

00:06:50.187 --> 00:06:53.639
The next day I get up, and
even if I don't feel inspired,

00:06:53.639 --> 00:06:57.021
I'll sit for those three
hours until I get the brain

00:06:57.021 --> 00:07:00.022
says okay, you must start
to produce something.

00:07:00.022 --> 00:07:04.002
So try to write quickly,
and I follow a pattern

00:07:04.002 --> 00:07:06.816
just the way you would if you
were in the Army or whatever.

00:07:06.816 --> 00:07:09.346
And then that gets me through it quickly.

00:07:09.346 --> 00:07:11.820
Some writers will take a year, two years,

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:13.158
everyone has a different process.

00:07:13.158 --> 00:07:14.019
But that's what I do.

00:07:14.019 --> 00:07:14.923
- [Interviewer] You write
better in the morning.

00:07:14.923 --> 00:07:16.119
- Much better.

00:07:16.119 --> 00:07:18.418
I use to write at night all the time,

00:07:18.418 --> 00:07:21.641
but I found that
everything, everyone died,

00:07:21.641 --> 00:07:23.167
it was dark, kind of creepy.

00:07:23.167 --> 00:07:24.785
(laughter)

00:07:24.785 --> 00:07:28.189
It was really horrible, very depressing.

00:07:28.189 --> 00:07:30.045
Then I realized Edgar Allen
Poe used to write at night,

00:07:30.045 --> 00:07:32.002
and I figured out why.

00:07:32.002 --> 00:07:34.690
If you've never had anything, like today,

00:07:34.690 --> 00:07:37.445
I would go okay, I'm selling.

00:07:37.445 --> 00:07:39.425
I can't give up the house.

00:07:39.425 --> 00:07:40.494
But if you don't have the house,

00:07:40.494 --> 00:07:41.473
and you don't have anything,

00:07:41.473 --> 00:07:43.873
and you're just sitting
there and you go okay,

00:07:43.873 --> 00:07:45.874
I wrote this for myself
and I don't even know

00:07:45.874 --> 00:07:49.402
if it's that good, but
obviously someone likes it,

00:07:49.402 --> 00:07:50.802
I'll regret it.

00:07:50.802 --> 00:07:51.872
I know I'll regret it.

00:07:51.872 --> 00:07:56.255
I bet I say it once a week,
I walk around and I say,

00:07:56.255 --> 00:07:57.919
thank God, all the time.

00:07:57.919 --> 00:07:59.152
- Really?

00:07:59.152 --> 00:08:01.699
All the time, you did one thing right.

00:08:01.699 --> 00:08:03.940
In life you could do 1,000 things wrong,

00:08:03.940 --> 00:08:06.271
you just do one thing right, you go whew,

00:08:06.271 --> 00:08:09.479
it makes up for the other
999 mistakes sometimes.

00:08:09.479 --> 00:08:12.642
Give up something to get something,

00:08:12.642 --> 00:08:17.574
and I thought I didn't have
any money, so I had no phone.

00:08:17.574 --> 00:08:20.561
I painted my windows
black, so I didn't know

00:08:20.561 --> 00:08:23.107
if the sun was up, the
sun was down, all I knew

00:08:23.107 --> 00:08:24.690
was I had to write.

00:08:26.274 --> 00:08:29.952
And I wrote, and I wrote, it was very bad.

00:08:29.952 --> 00:08:34.119
It wasn't good at all, but
every day it was getting better

00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:38.945
and better, like a machine, like a muscle,

00:08:38.945 --> 00:08:42.028
until finally the idea of Rocky came.

00:08:43.312 --> 00:08:47.479
So the idea is you must fail
100 times to succeed once,

00:08:50.192 --> 00:08:52.343
that's part of it.

00:08:52.343 --> 00:08:54.911
No one succeeds the first time.

00:08:54.911 --> 00:08:59.446
It's learning how to not get
disappointed with failure

00:08:59.446 --> 00:09:00.933
to understand failure.

00:09:00.933 --> 00:09:03.876
You only learn when you fail,

00:09:03.876 --> 00:09:05.943
you don't learn when you succeed.

00:09:05.943 --> 00:09:09.360
But when you fail, that's when you learn.

00:09:10.338 --> 00:09:15.195
How I learned, lucky I
was a failure in New York,

00:09:15.195 --> 00:09:17.035
and that is the reason I started writing,

00:09:17.035 --> 00:09:19.005
which made me successful.

00:09:19.005 --> 00:09:23.289
I had to teach myself
early on, expect to fail,

00:09:23.289 --> 00:09:27.188
expect it, covet it, welcome
it, it's going to happen.

00:09:27.188 --> 00:09:30.355
But don't, it's not a permanent state.

00:10:30.073 --> 00:10:31.396
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:10:31.396 --> 00:10:34.671
I made this video because
Daniel "Danny" Frankel

00:10:34.671 --> 00:10:36.851
asked me to, so if there
is a famous entrepreneur

00:10:36.851 --> 00:10:38.684
that you want me to profile next,

00:10:38.684 --> 00:10:40.272
leave it in the comments
below, and I'll see

00:10:40.272 --> 00:10:41.485
what I can do.

00:10:41.485 --> 00:10:43.789
I'd also love to know
which of Sly Stallone's

00:10:43.789 --> 00:10:46.185
10 rules mean the most to you,

00:10:46.185 --> 00:10:48.672
is the most applicable to
your life and your business.

00:10:48.672 --> 00:10:49.868
Leave it in the comments below,

00:10:49.868 --> 00:10:51.622
I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:10:51.622 --> 00:10:52.773
Thank you so much for watching,

00:10:52.773 --> 00:10:56.356
continue to believe,
and I'll see you soon.

